<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Corcu Duibne</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Corcu Duibne were an early medieval Irish population group of west Munster, settled on the Dingle and Iveragh peninsulas of modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/county-kerry">County Kerry</a>. Counted among the Érainn, they were later subject to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eoganachta">Eóganacht</a> kings of Iarmumu. Their territory holds one of the densest concentrations of ogham stones, many naming members of the group.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/corcu-duibne.jpg" alt="Corcu Duibne" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jim · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dunbeg_Fort,_Dingle_Peninsula,_Kerry,_Ireland..jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The name means &quot;seed or tribe of Duibhne&quot;, the name of a goddess, and ogham inscriptions show that the Corcu Duibne claimed descent from a female ancestor, DOVINIA. They also claimed descent from Conaire Mór, a figure of legend, which would make them distant kin of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dal-riata">Dál Riata</a>, the Múscraige and the Corcu Baiscind, all of the Síl Conairi of legend and ultimately of the Clanna Dedad.</p><p>The 8th-century text The Expulsion of the Déisi gives them a founder, Corc Duibne. In its later version Corc and his twin Cormac are born of incest to Coirpre Músc and Duihind, the crops fail, and a druid and his wife Boí take Corc to the island of Inis Boí. There, after a year of morning purifications on the back of an otherworldly white cow with red ears, his curse passes into the cow, which leaps into the sea and becomes the rock of Bó Boí. As a hostage at the court of Cormac mac Airt, King of Tara, Corc is fostered by Óengus Gaíbúaibthech of the Déisi, follows him into exile, and finally stays on his old island to found his dynasty.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The Corcu Duibne are attested as early as the 5th century, and more than a third of all Irish ogham inscriptions come from their region. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a> hill fortress of Caherconree on the Dingle Peninsula keeps the name of Cú Roí, a cousin of Conaire Mór in legend, and the peninsula's Modern Irish name is Corca Dhuibhne. Their relations with the Eóganacht Locha Léin are poorly understood, but they seem to have spent at least some time under its overlordship in the Kingdom of Iarmuman; rule from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eoganacht-chaisil">Eóganacht Chaisil</a> is not apparent. Their ruling clans were the O'Shea, O'Falvey and O'Connell.</p><p>The annals record their kings from the late 10th century: Congal son of Anrudán died in 989, Ua Ségda was slain in 1041, and Mathgamain Ua Ségda died in 1096. In 1064 Tairdelbach made a great raid into their country, taking <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a> beyond counting, and in 1118 Tadc Ua Ségda was slain by the foreigners of Luimnech.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Corcu Duibne” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Early Irish tradition remembers the Érainn as an ancient people of Munster, ancestors of groups such as the Corcu Loigde and the <b>Corcu Duibne</b>, who were powerful before the rise of the Eóganachta.</blockquote><a href="/state/iverni">Iverni</a></li><li><blockquote>Their territory covered much of later Kerry and west Cork, and they ruled over earlier peoples such as the <b>Corcu Duibne</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-iarmumu">Kingdom of Iarmumu</a></li><li><blockquote>Their region later formed part of the Kingdom of Munster and the lands of peoples such as the <b>Corcu Duibne</b> and the Ciarraige Luachra.</blockquote><a href="/state/velabri">Velabri</a></li></ul></section>
